                <pb facs="0136" n="56" xml:id="pg56"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Hay to Mr. McCormick.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1902-03-06"><hi rendition="#italic">March 6,
                        1902</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 35.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have to acknowledge the receipt of your
                    No. 67 of the 11th ultimo, in regard to the treatment to be accorded to United
                    States passports and their bearers in Austria-Hungary.</p>
                <p>Your conduct of the matter, as reported in your despatch, has the Department’s
                    approval, as has the fifth article of the proposed instruction to the
                    Austro-Hungarian local authorities, requiring a receipt from them in every case
                    in which they deem it necessary temporarily to take from an American citizen his
                    passport.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">* * * * * * *</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John Hay.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
